'''D-O''' was a custom data storage and retrieval [[droid]] that was once owned by the [[Sith assassin]] [[Ochi]] of [[Bestoon]].<ref name="Galactic Guide">''[[Star Wars: The Rise of Skywalker: The Galactic Guide]]''</ref> The droid was created by a [[Unidentified droidsmith|droidsmith]], who was killed by Ochi.<ref name="TROSVD" /> The droid was reactivated by [[BB-8]] in [[35 ABY]], on [[Pasaana]], and later brought along to [[Kijimi]], where he was further repaired by [[Rey Skywalker|Rey]].<ref name="Episode IX" /> An excitable little droid, D-O befriended the [[Resistance]] droid, and wanted to be just like his droid friend.<ref name="Amazing Sticker Adventures">''[[Star Wars: The Rise of Skywalker: Amazing Sticker Adventures]]''</ref>

D-O spoke a less complex form of droid [[binary]] that was easier for non-droids to understand than most droid speech<ref name="TROSVD" /> and was capable of speaking in [[Galactic Basic Standard|Basic]], though it was rather limited and often times garbled, with a noticeable stutter.<ref name="Episode IX" /> D-O's cone was equipped with a [[Cold box/Canon|cold box]] storage unit, distinct from his [[droid brain]], allowing him to store sensitive information without being able to access it.<ref name="SWE" />

==Biography==
===Unwilling aid of Ochi===
[[File:OchiIsASquare-CEUEEd.png|thumb|right|200px|D-O served the assassin Ochi after he killed his old master.]]
D-O was a [[droid]], who at sometime during or prior to [[21 ABY]], was constructed by a [[Unidentified droidsmith|droidsmith]]. However, D-O's creator perished at the hands of the [[Sith]] [[assassin]] [[Ochi|Ochi of Bestoon]], who after killing the [[droidsmith]], took D-O for himself. D-O was kept aboard Ochi's ship, the ''[[Bestoon Legacy]]''. During his time in service of Ochi, D-O gained an abundance of files and data on the Sith world of [[Exegol]] in the [[Unknown Regions]], and the mysterious [[Sith Eternal]], the group that Ochi served. D-O was also treated poorly by his master.<ref name="Episode IX" />

On a mission to [[Pasaana]], Ochi, while being tracked by [[Jedi]] [[Luke Skywalker]] and [[General]] [[Landonis Balthazar Calrissian|Lando Calrissian]], became trapped in quicksand, and perished, leaving his ship abandoned in the desert, with D-O deactivated aboard.<ref name="Episode IX" />

===Reactivation===
[[File:Rey_on_Pasaana_Festival.jpg|thumb|left|280px|D-O became friends with the Resistance members.]]
Years later, in [[35 ABY]], a team consisting of the Jedi [[Rey Skywalker|Rey]], a former [[First Order]] [[First Order stormtrooper|stormtrooper]] [[Finn]], the [[pilot]] [[Poe Dameron]], the droids [[BB-8]] and [[C-3PO]] and the [[Wookiee]] [[Chewbacca]] [[Mission to Pasaana (Resistance)|journeyed]] to Pasaana, following the trail left by Luke from his search for a [[Emperor's wayfinder|Sith wayfinder]], an [[Sith wayfinder|object]] the [[Resistance]] now needed to prevent the grand plan of [[Darth Sidious]] and the Sith Eternal from reaching fruition, who had been secretly amassing a massive fleet of [[Xyston-class Star Destroyer|''Xyston''-class Star Destroyers]], the [[Final Order]], on Exegol, which required a wayfinder to locate, as the way to the Sith world was treacherous and uncharted. While exploring Ochi's derelict ship, BB-8 stumbled across the deactivated D-O, left alone and covered in dust. BB-8 promptly reactivated the little droid, who expressed his gratitude at being returned to life. However, when Rey reached out to touch him, the little droid backed away, not wanting to be touched, leading the Jedi to reach the conclusion that the previous owner, Ochi, had treated him badly. Rey convinced the droid it was alright, and he was now with them.<ref name="Episode IX" />

[[File:D-O-kijimi.jpg|thumb|right|280px|D-O joins the Resistance on Kijimi]]
D-O joined his new friends on a trip to [[Kijimi]], seeking the droidsmith [[Babu Frik]] to allow C-3PO to read an inscription written on [[Blade of Ochi of Bestoon|Ochi's dagger]], which was kept in the droids memory. While on Kijimi, Rey learned Chewbacca was alive, and alongside Poe, Finn and BB-8, D-O traveled to the [[Resurgent-class Star Destroyer|''Resurgent''-class Star Destroyer]] [[Steadfast (Resurgent-class)|''Steadfast'']]. While they got separated, leading to the capture of Finn and Poe, who had just freed Chewbacca, they were saved by General [[Armitage Hux]], who had betrayed the First Order. D-O alerted Threepio that their friends were ahead, and boarded the ''[[Millennium Falcon]]'' alongside them, with Hux staying behind.<ref name="Episode IX" />

Following their escape from the ''Steadfast'', the ''Falcon'' endured a rough landing on [[Kef Bir]], the location of the wayfinder as written on the dagger. It led to the [[Death Star ruins|ruins]] of [[DS-2 Death Star II Mobile Battle Station|second Death Star]]. D-O, curious asked what the wreck was, with Rey telling him it was the Death Star, ruins from an old war. After locating where the [[wayfinder]] was in the ruins by using the dagger, they were interrupted by the arrival of [[Company 77]], who wanted by to know what business the Resistance had on their moon. D-O curiously investigated the [[orbak]]s, but was frightened by them. After Rey left the others behind, D-O stated that he missed her. Finn asked the droid his name, but then learned that D-O had a lot of information on Exegol, which was Ochi's intended destination before his death.<ref name="Episode IX" />

The information kept by D-O, crossed with a live transmission sent by Rey piloting Luke Skywalker's [[T-65B X-wing starfighter|T-65B X-wing]] [[Red Five (T-65B X-wing starfighter)|''Red Five'']], allowed the Resistance to locate Exegol and the Sith fleet, giving them a fighting chance at stopping the destruction that could be wrought. After a hard fought battle, and the arrival of the [[Citizens' Fleet]], the Sith Eternal was defeated, and the Emperor vanquished once and done for all. D-O joined the celebrations with his new friends, never again to be a slave to evil.<ref name="Episode IX" />

==Behind the scenes==
{{Quote|For instance, with D-O we were told he should be like a little duckling that has hatched and latched onto BB-8 as a sort of mother figure. But the focus that character got over the course of his design was intense. We submitted dozens and dozens of ideas. It took months to get to the point where it was a wheel with that cone head. I did variations on the end of his snout, the dimensions of the cone, the volume of his head, how wide it was, the tire treads.|[[Jake Lunt Davies]]|<ref name="Lunt Davies 224">{{InsiderCite|224|Jake Lunt Davies' Star Wars Sketchbook|Jake Lunt Davies' ''Star Wars'' Sketchbook}}</ref>}}
D-O appeared in the [[2019]] film [[Star Wars: Episode IX The Rise of Skywalker|''Star Wars'': Episode IX ''The Rise of Skywalker'']], the final installment of the ''[[Star Wars]]'' [[sequel trilogy]], where he was voiced by director [[J.J. Abrams]] and puppeteered by Robin Guiver and Lynn Robertson Bruce. The droid made its debut at the Episode IX panel during [[Celebration Chicago]].<ref name="Episode IX panel">{{SWYouTube|RnhiLZOprZE|Star Wars: Episode IX Panel &#124; Star Wars Celebration Chicago 2019}}</ref> [[Lucasfilm]] sound editor [[Matthew Wood]] took over the voice role in the [[2020]] animated movie ''[[The LEGO Star Wars Holiday Special]]''.<ref name="Holiday Special">''[[The LEGO Star Wars Holiday Special]]''</ref>

The droid was partially inspired by ducklings.<ref name="TROSVD" /> Another visual reference was the comic ''Spy vs. Spy''. In terms of personality, D-O was based on Terrio's dog George, whom Terrio had rescued from an abusive owner.<ref name="Art of TROS" /> Originally, Abrams' voice was supposed to be temporary, but screenwriter [[Chris Terrio]] watched a few cuts with Abrams' voice as D-O and felt that the anxious politeness in Abrams' performance was perfect for the droid. Other actors were considered, but Terrio convinced the director to keep the voice.<ref>{{WebCite|url=https://www.vanityfair.com/hollywood/2019/12/star-wars-the-rise-of-skywalker-cameos-who-were-the-jedi-voices-jodie-comer-reys-mom/amp|text=25 ''Star Wars: The Rise of Skywalker'' Cameos You Might Have Missed|author=Anthony Breznican, Joanna Robinson|date=2019-12-19|quote="J.J. didn't want to leave it as his voice," screenwriter '''Chris Terrio''' told ''Vanity Fair''. "His voice was just in there as temp, and I was convinced after watching a few cuts with J.J.'s voice as D-O, that J.J. just was D-O. There was no other possibility." Other actors were considered, but Terrio won the argument to keep it as-is because Abrams' anxious politeness cannot be replicated. "There's something about the way that J.J. pronounces some of those words, like 'No, thank you,' and, 'Very kind, thank you.' They're so J.J. and so D-O, so that that stuck around," Terrio said.|archivedate=20200223085926}}</ref>

Prior to Episode IX's release, ''[[Ultimate Star Wars, New Edition]]'' stated that D-O was created by Babu Frik;<ref name="Ultimate Star Wars" /> however, the film itself revealed that was not the case.<ref name="Episode IX" /> ''[[Star Wars: The Rise of Skywalker: The Visual Dictionary]]'', a reference book accompanying the film, revealed that D-O was created by an unnamed droidsmith who was [[Death|killed]] by Ochi.<ref name="TROSVD" /> D-O belonging to Babu Frik was from an early version of the script, where BB-8 would repair the broken D-O in Frik's workshop, and the droid would imprint on BB-8.<ref name="Art of TROS">''[[The Art of Star Wars: The Rise of Skywalker]]''</ref>

